<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Opium Wars</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1839">1839</a>–<a href="/years/1860">1860</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Opium Wars were two conflicts between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a> and Western powers, chiefly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, in 1839–1842 and 1856–1860. Arising from British efforts to force open Chinese markets, including to the opium trade, they ended in Qing defeat. The resulting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unequal-treaties">unequal treaties</a> ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> and established foreign privileges that weakened the Qing Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/opium-wars.jpg" alt="Opium Wars" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edward Duncan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Destroying_Chinese_war_junks,_by_E._Duncan_(1843).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the century of naval dominance that followed, Britain suppressed the Atlantic slave trade through the West Africa Squadron, forced open China in the <b>Opium Wars</b>, annexed large territories across Africa and Asia, and maintained imperial communications across the globe without fear of blockade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Chinese markets were forced open through the <b>Opium Wars</b> and the unequal treaty system, which granted foreign powers extraterritorial jurisdiction and treaty ports, fixed low import tariffs and ceded Hong Kong Island to Britain in 1842.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Opium Wars</b> (1839–1860), the British conquest of India by 1858 and the French conquests in Africa, Polynesia and Indochina by 1887, vast populations of Asia became consumers of European exports, and colonies in Africa yielded rubber, diamonds and coal.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/capitalism">Capitalism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>…American colonies in 1776; it turned to India, Australia, Asia, Africa and the Pacific, and after 1815 enjoyed a century of almost unchallenged dominance, the Pax Britannica, which was still an almost uninterrupted series of colonial wars, from the Anglo-Burmese and <b>Opium Wars</b> to the Scramble for Africa.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/imperialism">Imperialism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>When China opened its borders to foreign merchants and visitors after its defeat in the First and Second <b>Opium Wars</b>, the wall became a main attraction for tourists, and the travelogues of the later 19th century enhanced its reputation and its mythology.</blockquote><a href="/work/great-wall-of-china">Great Wall of China · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
